window.__labLoaded({"id":"3.2","title":"The Binomial Distribution","goal":"Use the binomial distribution for the lesson's drug that works for 70% of patients (10 patients): exactly, at most and at least, plus mean, SD and a simulation check.","question":"A drug works for 70% of patients. A clinic gives it to 10 patients. The doctor asks: how likely is it that exactly 7 respond, that at most 6 respond, and that at least 9 respond? What is the average number of responders and how much does it vary from clinic to clinic? Can the binomial distribution answer all of this, and does a simulation agree?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Exactly 7 respond","text":"binom.pmf(k, n, p) gives P(X = k), one bar of the distribution. We also compute the lesson's formula C(n, k) x p^k x (1 - p)^(n - k) by hand to see that they agree.","code":"import math\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import binom\n\nn, p = 10, 0.7\n\nby_hand = math.comb(n, 7) * p**7 * (1 - p)**3\nprint(f'formula   : {by_hand:.4f}')\nprint(f'binom.pmf : {binom.pmf(7, n, p):.4f}')","explain":["Load math for the combination function.","Load NumPy, which we will need for the simulation later.","Load the binomial distribution from SciPy.","Store the number of patients n and the chance of response p.","Compute the lesson's formula by hand: choose which 7 respond, then multiply their chances and the 3 failures' chances.","Print the hand formula.","Print SciPy's pmf, which gives P(X = 7) directly. The two numbers should match."],"meaning":"Both ways give P(X = 7) = 0.2668. About 27% of the time, exactly 7 of 10 patients respond. The hand formula and the SciPy function agree, so we can trust the function for harder questions.","out":"formula   : 0.2668\nbinom.pmf : 0.2668","figs":[]},{"title":"At most, more than, at least","text":"cdf(k) is P(X <= k), 'at most k'. sf(k) is the survival function P(X > k), 'more than k'. So 'at least 9' is sf(8), which is the same as 1 minus cdf(8).","code":"print(f'P(X <= 6)  at most 6      = {binom.cdf(6, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X > 8)   more than 8      = {binom.sf(8, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X >= 9)  at least 9       = {1 - binom.cdf(8, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(9) + P(10) added by hand  = {binom.pmf(9, n, p) + binom.pmf(10, n, p):.4f}')","explain":["P(at most 6): cdf(k) adds the probabilities of 0 up to k.","P(more than 8): sf(k) is the survival function, the chance of strictly more than k.","P(at least 9) is 1 minus the chance of 8 or fewer.","Check by adding P(9) and P(10) by hand. Three methods should agree."],"meaning":"P(X <= 6) = 0.3504, so a bit over a third of clinics see 6 or fewer responders. P(X > 8) and P(X >= 9) are both 0.1493, and P(9) + P(10) is also 0.1493. Rather few clinics (about 15%) would see 9 or more.","out":"P(X <= 6)  at most 6      = 0.3504\nP(X > 8)   more than 8      = 0.1493\nP(X >= 9)  at least 9       = 0.1493\nP(9) + P(10) added by hand  = 0.1493","figs":[]},{"title":"Mean and standard deviation","text":"The shortcuts are mean = n x p and SD = square root of n x p x (1 - p). SciPy's stats method returns the same values from the distribution itself.","code":"print(f'mean = n*p = {n * p:.1f}')\nprint(f'variance   = {n * p * (1 - p):.2f}')\nprint(f'SD         = {math.sqrt(n * p * (1 - p)):.3f}')\n\nm, v = binom.stats(n, p)\nprint(f'scipy      : mean {float(m):.1f}, variance {float(v):.2f}')","explain":["The mean of a binomial is n x p.","The variance is n x p x (1 - p).","The SD is the square root of the variance, back in the units of patients.","Ask SciPy for the mean and variance of the distribution itself.","Print what SciPy returns, to check against our shortcuts."],"meaning":"The mean is 7.0 responders, the variance is 2.10 and the SD is 1.449. SciPy returns the same mean and variance. A typical clinic sees 7 responders, give or take about 1.4.","out":"mean = n*p = 7.0\nvariance   = 2.10\nSD         = 1.449\nscipy      : mean 7.0, variance 2.10","figs":[]},{"title":"Check by simulation","text":"Run the 10-patient trial 100,000 times with a seeded generator and count how many responders each trial has. The simulated shares should be close to the exact probabilities.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nresponders = rng.binomial(n, p, size=100_000)\n\nprint(f'simulated P(X = 7)  = {(responders == 7).mean():.4f}  (exact {binom.pmf(7, n, p):.4f})')\nprint(f'simulated P(X >= 9) = {(responders >= 9).mean():.4f}  (exact {binom.sf(8, n, p):.4f})')\nprint(f'simulated mean {responders.mean():.3f}, SD {responders.std():.3f}')","explain":["Create a seeded generator so the simulation repeats.","Run the 10-patient trial 100,000 times and record each trial's number of responders.","Print the simulated chance of exactly 7 next to the exact value.","Print the simulated chance of 9 or more next to the exact value.","Print the simulated mean and SD."],"meaning":"The simulation gave P(X = 7) = 0.2679 (exact 0.2668), P(X >= 9) = 0.1485 (exact 0.1493), a mean of 6.997 and an SD of 1.450. All are close to the exact answers, which shows the formulas describe what really happens.","out":"simulated P(X = 7)  = 0.2679  (exact 0.2668)\nsimulated P(X >= 9) = 0.1485  (exact 0.1493)\nsimulated mean 6.997, SD 1.450","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"n <- 10; p <- 0.7\ndbinom(7, n, p)                       # exactly 7\npbinom(6, n, p)                       # at most 6\npbinom(8, n, p, lower.tail = FALSE)   # more than 8 = at least 9\nn * p\nsqrt(n * p * (1 - p))\n\nset.seed(42)\nx <- rbinom(100000, n, p)\nmean(x == 7)","explain":["Store the number of patients and the response chance.","dbinom gives P(X = 7).","pbinom gives P(X <= 6).","lower.tail = FALSE gives the chance of more than 8, which is at least 9.","The mean is n x p.","The SD is the square root of n x p x (1 - p).","Fix the seed.","Simulate 100,000 trials of 10 patients.","Share of simulated trials with exactly 7 responders."],"expect":"P(X = 7) = 0.2668; P(X <= 6) = 0.3504; P(X >= 9) = 0.1493; mean 7; sd 1.449; simulated P(X = 7) close to 0.2668","read":"The output shows 0.2668 for exactly 7, 0.3504 for at most 6, 0.1493 for at least 9, then the mean 7 and the SD 1.449. The last number is the simulated share of 7s, close to 0.2668.","note":"dbinom, pbinom and rbinom are R's pmf, cdf and random draws. lower.tail = FALSE gives the survival probability P(X > k). The simulation is close to the exact value, not identical to Python's."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (Function group: PDF & Noncentral PDF, CDF & Noncentral CDF, Random Numbers)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE exact7 = PDF.BINOM(7, 10, 0.7).\nCOMPUTE atmost6 = CDF.BINOM(6, 10, 0.7).\nCOMPUTE atleast9 = 1 - CDF.BINOM(8, 10, 0.7).\nCOMPUTE mean_x = 10 * 0.7.\nCOMPUTE sd_x = SQRT(10 * 0.7 * 0.3).\nFORMATS exact7 atmost6 atleast9 sd_x (F8.4).\nLIST VARIABLES=exact7 atmost6 atleast9 mean_x sd_x.","explain":["Make a tiny data set so there is one row to compute in.","Start the data block.","One row containing the number 1.","End the data block.","PDF.BINOM(k, n, p) is the probability of exactly 7 responders.","CDF.BINOM(k, n, p) is the chance of 6 or fewer.","At least 9 is 1 minus the chance of 8 or fewer.","The mean is n x p.","The SD is the square root of n x p x (1 - p).","Show four decimals.","Print the results."],"read":"In the LIST output, exact7 is 0.2668, atmost6 is 0.3504, atleast9 is 0.1493, mean_x is 7 and sd_x is 1.4491. They match Python.","note":"PDF.BINOM(k, n, p) is the pmf and CDF.BINOM(k, n, p) is P(X <= k). For a simulation use COMPUTE x = RV.BINOM(10, 0.7) on many cases; the result is close to, not identical to, Python's."},"stata":{"code":"display binomialp(10, 7, 0.7)\ndisplay binomial(10, 6, 0.7)\ndisplay binomialtail(10, 9, 0.7)\ndisplay 10 * 0.7\ndisplay sqrt(10 * 0.7 * 0.3)\n\nclear\nset seed 42\nset obs 100000\ngen x = rbinomial(10, 0.7)\ngen is7 = (x == 7)\nsummarize is7","explain":["binomialp(n, k, p) is the chance of exactly 7 of 10.","binomial(n, k, p) is the chance of 6 or fewer.","binomialtail(n, k, p) is the chance of 9 or more.","The mean is n x p.","The SD is the square root of n x p x (1 - p).","Clear memory.","Fix the seed.","Make 100,000 empty rows, one per trial.","Simulate the number of responders in each trial of 10 patients.","Mark the trials with exactly 7 responders.","Summarize; the mean of the 0/1 variable is the share of trials with 7."],"read":"The first five lines print .2668, .3504, .1493, 7 and 1.4491. The last summarize gives the simulated share of exactly-7 trials, which is close to .2668 but not identical to Python's 0.2679.","note":"In Stata binomialp(n, k, p) is P(X = k), binomial(n, k, p) is P(X <= k) and binomialtail(n, k, p) is P(X >= k), so 'at least 9' uses k = 9. The simulated mean of is7 is close to 0.2668, not identical to Python's."},"conclusion":"With 10 patients and a 70% response rate, exactly 7 respond about 26.7% of the time, 6 or fewer respond 35.0% of the time, and 9 or more respond 14.9% of the time. The average is 7 responders with an SD of 1.45. The caution: the binomial assumes every patient has the same 70% chance independently, which real patients may not.","practice":{"scenario":"A multiple-choice quiz has 12 questions, each with 4 options and only one correct. A student who has not studied guesses every answer, so each guess is right with probability 0.25. The teacher wants to know how likely a guesser is to do well or very badly.","data":"n = 12 questions, p = 0.25 chance of a correct guess on each. For the simulation use the seed 2 and 100,000 quizzes.","tasks":["Find the chance of exactly 5 correct answers.","Find the chance of 8 or more correct, and the chance of 3 or fewer.","Find the mean and SD of the score.","Simulate 100,000 quizzes and compare the chance of 8 or more."],"hint":"This is a binomial with n = 12 and p = 0.25. Use binom.pmf for exactly, binom.sf(7, ...) for 8 or more and binom.cdf(3, ...) for 3 or fewer.","solution":{"code":"from scipy.stats import binom\nimport numpy as np\n\nn, p = 12, 0.25\nprint(f'P(X = 5)  = {binom.pmf(5, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X >= 8) = {binom.sf(7, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X <= 3) = {binom.cdf(3, n, p):.4f}')\nprint(f'mean = {n * p}, sd = {np.sqrt(n * p * (1 - p)):.3f}')\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(2)\nguesses = rng.binomial(n, p, size=100_000)\nprint(f'simulated P(X >= 8) = {(guesses >= 8).mean():.5f}')\nprint(f'simulated mean = {guesses.mean():.3f}')","answer":"P(X = 5) = 0.1032, P(X >= 8) = 0.0028 (fewer than 3 guessers in 1,000) and P(X <= 3) = 0.6488. The mean is 3.0 correct with an SD of 1.500. The simulation gave P(X >= 8) = 0.00267 and a mean of 3.002, in line with the exact values. Guessing almost never passes a 12-question quiz.","out":"P(X = 5)  = 0.1032\nP(X >= 8) = 0.0028\nP(X <= 3) = 0.6488\nmean = 3.0, sd = 1.500\nsimulated P(X >= 8) = 0.00267\nsimulated mean = 3.002","figs":[]}}});
